#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/id #uuid "77bb1db2-e2fb-5126-a116-fce07c309d0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7407" :question/problem "The growth rate of the hare population $P(t)$ in a particular forest is jointly proportional to the hare population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hare population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000$. How many hares will there be in the forest after $7$ years given that there were $2\\,500$ hares initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "Since the hare population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hare population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hare population, the hare population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality\n\n$$\nk=0.000\\,01\n$$\n\nand the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000$. Therefore, the hare population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.000\\,01 P(10\\,000-P)\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-0.1t}}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition\n\n$$\nP(0)=2\\,500\n$$\n\nand solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0) &= 2500 \\\\\n(10000)/(1 + Ce^{-0.1(0)}) &= 2500 \\\\\n\\frac{10000}{1 + C} &= 2500 \\\\\n\\frac{1 + C}{10000} &= \\frac{1}{2500} \\\\\n1 + C &= \\frac{10000}{2500} \\\\\n1 + C &= 4 \\\\\nC &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\frac{10\\,000}{1+3 e^{-0.1 t}}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of hares in the forest after $7$ years, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t)$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(7) &= \\frac{10000}{1 + 3e^{-0.1 \\cdot 7}} \\\\\n&\\approx 4016\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "e53b71f8-4ea8-50e7-81c6-62fbd2ac54ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-7408" :question/problem "The rate of growth of the panda population $P(t)$ in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model is\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.001\\,25 P(800-P)$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observations. Find an expression for the panda population after $t$ years, given that there were $400$ pandas initially." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is\n\n$$\nk=0.001\\,25\n$$\n\nand the carrying capacity is $a=800$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-(800\\cdot 0.001\\,25)t}}= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-t}}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition\n\n$$\nP(0)=400\n$$\n\nand solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{800}{1 + Ce^{-0}} &= 400 \\\\\n\\frac{800}{1 + C} &= 400 \\\\\n\\frac{1 + C}{800} &= \\frac{1}{400} \\\\\n1 + C &= \\frac{800}{400} \\\\\nC &= 2 - 1 \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the panda population is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)= \\dfrac{800}{1+e^{-t}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8691b2a6-c748-5021-b507-44c96ea3713d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179220} {:answer/id #uuid "88ba5a31-6b61-5f52-8f33-d6de20464f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4354" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179221} {:answer/id #uuid "aff57b8d-f271-5427-bd4a-35a20ddcdaad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4582" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179222} {:answer/id #uuid "b1583f25-d5d8-51b5-b04f-8efe2af554ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179223} {:answer/id #uuid "c370ed19-9e73-5f99-a4ed-a9f34eb6d2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c370ed19-9e73-5f99-a4ed-a9f34eb6d2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179224} :answer-field/id #uuid "3efd3de1-b598-582c-b19a-3c6dd81576b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179219}] :question/id #uuid "94524d73-2bf5-594f-b12c-32a707870543" :question/math-academy-id "q-83771" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.00001$ and the carrying capacity is $5000$. How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
